{"compatibility":"minor","safety_note_md":"# SafeStan Safety Note

Compatibility: minor.

Protected data: `y`.

Generated files: `model_safe.stan`, `data_safe.json`.

Changes: the protected observation already appears as the top-level sampling statement `y ~ normal(...)` and is not used before observation. The original program gave priors to `beta[1]`, `beta[2]`, and `beta[3]` using indexed left-hand sides; SafeStan requires one prior sampling statement on the top-level parameter. The safe model makes the model's hard-coded three-covariate slope-prior structure explicit by constraining `M` to 3 and replaces those three component priors with the equivalent vectorized prior `beta ~ normal(...)`. No data fields or values were added; `data_safe.json` is the supplied data unchanged.
","model_safe_stan":"data {
  int<lower=3, upper=3> M; // Number of covariates
  int<lower=0> N; // Number of observations

  vector[M] x0;   // Covariate baselines
  matrix[N, M] X; // Covariate design matrix

  array[N] real y;      // Variates
}

transformed data {
  matrix[N, M] deltaX;
  for (n in 1:N) {
    deltaX[n,] = X[n] - x0';
  }
}

parameters {
  real alpha;          // Intercept
  vector[M] beta;      // Slopes
  real<lower=0> sigma; // Measurement Variability
}

model {
  // Prior model
  alpha ~ normal(3000, 1000 / 2.33);
  beta ~ normal([0, 0, 0]', [200 / 2.33, 20 / 2.33, 4 / 2.33]');
  sigma ~ normal(0, 500);

  // Vectorized observation model
  y ~ normal(alpha + deltaX * beta, sigma);
}

// Simulate a full observation from the current value of the parameters
generated quantities {
  array[N] real y_pred = normal_rng(alpha + deltaX * beta, sigma);
}
","data_safe_json":"{"M":3,"N":2,"X":[[3.4565887095014354,5.167787887169031,0.30000000000000004],[6.4303886023027435,4.462795188624792,0.6000000000000001]],"x0":[4.477950784719392,4.167147125394697,0.30000000000000004],"y":[2.0,2.0]}","protected_variables":["y"]}
